The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". Its own table says
otherwise. This page splits #4471 into the problems it holds, one ticket each.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. #4471 holds three problems with three different fixes. Fixing the parser alone
leaves the other two open.
</Callout>

## The problems

<SplitCounts ticket={4471} {issues} />

- **parser** — the shared parser reads dates month first; every tenant here writes the day first,
  so day and month swap. Fix: parse in the tenant's locale.
- **hint** — booking-v3's form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field. People type month first,
  as told, and the form reads day first. Same symptom, different bug: the hint, not the parser.
  Fix: a hint (or picker) that follows the tenant's locale.
- **one-day move** — the reports the desk already noted in `desk-notes.json`. A booking moves by one
  day ("12 May → 11 May", "Lisbon got Tuesday, Toronto got Monday"). A day↔month swap cannot do
  that, so they are counted on their own. Looks like a time-zone shift.

## By cause, as the kb computes it

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table files the one-day moves under `parser` by tenant and footnotes them; the list above
counts them apart.

## How this was found

- The export has duplicate rows where its pages overlap; counted once by id.
- Each report is placed by its **tenant** (locale and booking form) and channel, with
  `causeOf` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts`. Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser and
  often disagrees with the cause (see "tagged as" above).
- Every dated report body was checked: all are an exact day↔month swap, except the one-day moves.
- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone. Subjects here are
  shared across all three problems, so subject-only merging cannot keep them apart.

## Status

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: **parser** is fixed (`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`),
**hint** is won't fix (the booking-v3 form is retired). The parser fix does not move a date by one
day, so the **one-day move** reports stay open on their own ticket.

Written by Enrique Millán, with Claude.

